exports[`dg guide の出力（本文の組み立て） > トピックごとの本文と、節の本文が変わらない > overview 1`] = `
"# How to use deguboard (for agents)

deguboard is a ticket tool that is managed with Git. You (the AI agent) call \`dg\` from Bash to read and write tickets. Humans decide "what to build", "in what order to proceed", and "whether to approve the deliverables". You carry out filing, planning, implementation, and review.

## Rules to follow every time

- Always call \`dg\` with \`--json\` and read the result's \`ok\` and \`data\`. A failure has \`error.code\` and \`error.message\`. Exit codes: 0 success / 1 failure / 2 usage error
- Always pass \`--actor agent\` on every call (environment variables may not persist across Bash calls). Operations limited to humans are refused for agents (\`TRANSITION_REJECTED\`). When refused, ask a human
- You are recorded as \`<user>/agent\` (for example \`eiki/agent\`). The user is decided in this order: \`--user\`, \`DEGUBOARD_USER\`, git config \`deguboard.user\`, git \`user.name\`, OS user name (the source is shown as \`user_source\` in \`whoami\`). A human is recorded as \`<user>\`. A ticket's creator (\`created_by\`) and assignee (\`assignee\`) are stored in this form. The \`--created-by\` and \`--assignee\` options also accept the shorthands \`human\` (the user) and \`agent\` (the user's AI)
- Do not edit data files directly (under \`.git/deguboard/\`). Read them with \`dg … show --json\`. Changes written outside dg are found the next time dg runs, and dg stops writing (see "Changes outside dg" below)
- Ticket IDs can be \`t12\` or \`12\`. Epics are \`e3\`, Iterations are \`s3\`. When calling from outside the repository, use \`-C <dir>\`
- Writes are committed according to \`commit.mode\` (currently \`batch\`). Filing, claim, and starting or reopening an Iteration commit and push at once. In \`batch\`, other changes stay in the working tree, so run \`dg --json --actor agent sync\` at each break. In \`manual\`, \`sync\` does not commit either, so ask a human to run \`dg commit\` (only \`sync --resolve\`, which resolves conflicts, commits the waiting changes first, even in \`manual\`)
- Record work results as a Run. When the parent advances work itself, \`ticket claim\` opens the implementation Run, so note the ID in \`data.run\`, and close it when recording the PR (\`ticket update --pr\`). For the steps, see the "When the parent advances work itself" section of \`dg guide run\`. Usage (token counts) is imported from the conversation record when the Run closes, so do not write the numbers yourself
- If a filing result has \`provisional: true\`, the number is provisional and not yet pushed, and the ID may change. Tell a human and run \`sync\`
- Write tickets, PRs, commits, and replies to humans in the language of the repository's existing text and of the human's request. Do not switch to English just because this guide is in English

## When sync stops

Writes and \`sync\` take in the remote deguboard branch before they push. When they stop, do the following. In every case, do not edit the data files.

- **Conflict**: \`data.conflicts\` of \`sync\` is not empty (\`ok\` stays true), or filing, claim, or starting or reopening an Iteration fails with \`SYNC_CONFLICT\`. Both sides changed the same field to different values, or changed the same line of a body. Local commits remain, and push, reconcile, filing, claim, and starting or reopening an Iteration stop until it is resolved. Do not choose which value to keep yourself (even when advancing with \`--auto\`). Show the files in \`conflicts\` and ask a human with choices: "remote" (take the remote value) or "local" (take the local value). Then run \`dg --json --actor agent sync --resolve <remote|local>\` once, on the side that was chosen. Only the conflicting fields (for a body, the lines that collided) take that side's value. Other changes and commits remain, and push and reconcile complete. Tell a human the \`file\`, \`field\`, \`taken\` (the value taken), and \`discarded\` (the value thrown away) in \`data.resolved\`. If \`conflicts\` remains after \`--resolve\`, the file is outside the merge driver's scope (such as \`config.yaml\`), so a human has to resolve it, as the warning's remedy says
- **Push keeps being refused**: if filing, claim, or starting or reopening an Iteration fails with \`PUSH_FAILED\`, nothing was fetched and no commit remains. Wait a little and retry the same operation (for claim, first check with \`ticket show\` that no one has started it). If another write leaves a warning that it could not be pushed, the commit remains locally, and the next \`sync\` sends it again
- **Remote unreachable**: the commits remain locally, and the filed number becomes a provisional number (\`provisional: true\`). When the remote is reachable again, run \`sync\`. If the provisional number collides with a remote number, \`sync\` renumbers it and shows \`{ from, to }\` in \`data.renumbered\`. If it was renumbered, correct the number you told a human and tell them again. When there is a conflict besides the provisional number, or when uncommitted changes remain in \`manual\`, it cannot be renumbered and appears as a conflict above (\`--resolve\` renumbers first, then commits the waiting changes and resolves the conflict)

## Changes outside dg

dg checks the data repository (the deguboard worktree) for changes that did not go through dg commands (uncommitted changes, and commits without dg's mark) each time a command runs. When it finds some, a command that writes stops with \`OUTSIDE_CHANGES\`, and a command that only reads returns a warning. \`error.detail\` (in parentheses for a warning) shows which files are involved (for a commit, the short SHA, subject, and files). If a Stop hook is installed, the same thing is returned when you finish a response.

- Do not revert or adopt them on your own, even if you may have written them. Tell a human the files and commits that came up, and let them decide whether to adopt or discard
- **Adopt**: when a human confirms they are correct, run \`dg --json --actor agent adopt\`. If there are no errors in the same check as \`dg validate\`, the current state becomes the baseline for later checks (uncommitted changes are committed as waiting changes by the next \`commit\` or \`sync\`). If there are errors, \`adopt\` does not adopt them and fails with \`VALIDATION_FAILED\`, so let a human fix them or decide to discard them
- **Discard**: a human reverts in the deguboard worktree. Even after the revert, the baseline may differ, for example when dg's waiting changes were reverted too, so run \`dg --json --actor agent adopt\` to make the current state the baseline before continuing

## Operations that may be limited to humans

By default, agents can run every operation. Limits are added in the settings.

- By command: a command listed in \`actor.human_only\` in \`config.yaml\` is refused for the agent. Current setting: none. Examples: \`plan.approve\` (limit approving a Plan to humans), \`epic.close\`, \`iteration.close\`
- By ticket transition: a transition whose Workflow \`transitions\` marks \`by: [human]\` (such as making a ticket abandoned, or moving it back from done) is refused for the agent. Check with \`dg --json --actor agent workflow show <id>\`
- Making a ticket abandoned, undoing done, and closing or reopening an Epic or Iteration are decisions for humans. Even when they are not refused, if no human has instructed them, confirm with a human before running them
- Approving a Plan (\`plan approve\`) is done only when a human has approved it, even when it is not refused (see \`dg guide plan\`)

## Topics

| Topic    | Skill     | Contents                                                                                                                                       | When to read                                                       |
| -------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| import   | dgimport  | Turns a conversation into tickets (decomposition, dependencies, Epics, importing drafts)                                                       | When requirements are settled and asked to "make tickets"          |
| assign   | dgassign  | Puts tickets into Iterations (sprints) and Epics                                                                                               | When asked to "put it in the sprint"                               |
| check    | dgcheck   | Shows findings from \`sync\`, \`validate\`, and \`lint\` with fix proposals in a table, and fixes them after approval                                | Before planning / when asked to "check"                            |
| plan     | dgplan    | Decides the order of advancing, records it in a Plan, and gets human approval (dependencies, \`--ready\`, \`plan create\`, \`plan approve\`)         | Before advancing several tickets                                   |
| work     | dgwork    | Advances one ticket (the Plan's order and scope, claim, worktree, checking acceptance criteria, PR, reconcile; also work that needs no PR)     | When starting work on a ticket                                     |
| run      | None      | The Run for when the parent advances work itself (claim and PR recording), and cleaning up a stopped Run (a helper read from work and review)  | When recording a Run or cleaning up a stopped Run                  |
| parallel | None      | Advances ready tickets of a Plan at the same time, with a subagent per ticket (limits, roles of parent and subagents, one ticket does not stop the others) | When several tickets are ready and subagents can be started |
| review   | None      | Quality gate before opening a PR and before merging (checks, acceptance criteria, review, no conflicts), and review by a separate subagent with a fix loop that has a limit | After opening a PR as a draft / before merging automatically |
| audit    | dgaudit   | Before release, reviews the whole repository by perspective and files the findings after approval (subagents; does not edit files)            | When all tickets in an Iteration or Epic are done                  |
| release  | dgrelease | Confirms all tickets in an Iteration are done, advances with the repository's release steps, and closes the Iteration                         | After review, when releasing                                       |
| done     | dgdone    | Cleanup after merge, confirming the remaining acceptance criteria, and closing a Plan                                                          | After a PR is merged                                               |
| all      | dgall     | Advances assign → check → plan → work → audit → release, taking the stage from the state (can resume with the same arguments)                  | When asked to "carry s3 through to the end"                        |

Read with \`dg guide <topic>\`. In Claude Code, the Skill that \`dg skill install\` installs (such as \`/dgimport\`) reads the topic. A Skill is optional. Without one, read \`dg guide <topic>\` and proceed with the same steps. In Claude Code, \`dg hooks install\` can also install hooks, and \`dg hooks check\` confirms whether they are installed, whether they work, and whether their input has the right shape (it warns when something is missing). For the list of commands, see \`dg --help\`. For each command's options, see \`dg <group> <operation> --help\`."
`;

exports[`dg guide の出力（本文の組み立て） > トピックごとの本文と、節の本文が変わらない > parallel 1`] = `
"# Advancing ready tickets in parallel (parallel)

When \`next\` returns \`implement\`, \`review\`, \`fix\`, or \`ready\` for several tickets, and subagents can be started, you may start one for each ticket and advance them at the same time. \`dg\` only shows the order. Starting and waiting for them is done by these steps. The session that advances (the parent) only advances, gets approvals, and reads and writes \`dg\`.

- **Limit**: up to 3 at the same time. If a human gives a number, follow it. If checks fail under load, reduce it
- **Before starting**: look at the open Plan and the PRs, and check that no two tickets change the same file. Do not run tickets that change the same file side by side. Propose to a human that they run in order with \`after\`
- **Parent**: for each ticket, do the \`implement\` step of the "Flow" section of \`dg guide work\`, and the "Before and after claim" section of \`dg guide work\`, one at a time (claim with \`--no-run\`; claim creates the worktree), and start a subagent. When it returns, do the "Receive the implementation result" section of \`dg guide work\`, and the review and fix loop in \`dg guide review\` (light findings you did not fix are added to the PR body by \`dg pr ready\`). With the free slot, read \`dg next\` again and start the next one. Tickets waiting for a merge and tickets waiting for a human do not count toward the slots
- **Passing the text**: pass the \`brief\` from \`dg next\` as it is (for fixes, the \`--role fix\` text). The steps of each role are already in the text, so do not have the subagent read the guide. The model is \`model\` from \`dg next\` (\`null\` means the parent's model)
- **The subagent does not**: claim, unclaim, \`iteration add\`, Plan operations, start a reviewer, \`dg pr ready\`, merge, \`ticket check\`, \`ticket update --pr\`, or work on other tickets
- **One ticket does not stop the others**: on \`CLAIM_LOST\`, skip that ticket. If implementation fails, \`ticket unclaim\` it back to todo, keep the worktree, and do not claim it again in the same flow. If a review or fix returns failure or does not return, follow the "How to finish" section of \`dg guide review\`. Wait only for tickets linked by \`after\`"
`;

exports[`dg guide の出力（本文の組み立て） > トピックごとの本文と、節の本文が変わらない > plan 1`] = `
"# Plan (plan)

Decides the order in which to advance several tickets. The plan is recorded in a Plan (\`p3\`). A Plan holds the order (\`after\`) and, for each ticket, the scope, approach, and tier (\`details\`'s \`scope\`, \`approach\`, and \`tier\`). It is created awaiting approval, and it becomes approved with \`plan approve\` after a human's approval.

## Deciding the target

- Ticket ID (one or more): creates a Plan for those tickets
- Iteration (\`s3\`) and Epic (\`e3\`): targets the todos in them (\`ticket list --iteration s3 --status todo\`)
- \`next [N]\`: the top N of \`ticket list --ready\` (one if omitted)
- Plan ID (\`p3\`): if awaiting approval, get approval with steps 6 and 7 below. If already approved, ask with the "Choosing the next step" section
- If asked only for the order, such as "What is next?", show the proposal from steps 1 to 4 and stop. Do not create a Plan
- No target given: suggests the todos of the active Iteration and confirms them

## Flow

1. Collect the targets: \`dg --json --actor agent ticket list --project default\` (narrow by Epic with \`--epic e3\`, or by Iteration with \`--iteration s3\`)
2. Check dependencies: each ticket's \`depends_on\`, and the \`dependents\` (tickets that depend on it) from \`dg --json --actor agent ticket show <id>\`
3. Find tickets that can start: \`dg --json --actor agent ticket list --ready\`. These are todos whose dependencies are all done or abandoned (\`deps_done\`). This does not check the assignee, so avoid tickets whose \`assignee\` is another user (if only your own, use \`--assignee agent\`)
4. Make an order proposal. Dependencies come first, and higher priority comes first. Show the tickets that can run in parallel (those that do not depend on each other) together. If a ticket's body has a \`## References\` section (or the Japanese \`## 参考\`) and the files written there overlap with another ticket's references, do not run them in parallel. Connect them with \`after\` (put the one that can advance first in front). Tickets without a references section are not judged for this overlap, and their order is decided by dependencies and priority as before. References are an estimate at filing time, so state the overlapping files in the proposal, and let a human fix it at approval
5. Record it in a Plan awaiting approval (see the "Creating a Plan" section). The result's \`data.id\` is the Plan's ID
6. Show it to a human and get approval. Show \`items\` (the Plan's order), \`after\`, and \`details\` from \`dg --json --actor agent plan show <p>\` in a table (include a tier column), and ask with the choices "Approve", "Revise", and "Keep it awaiting approval". If asked to revise, close it with \`plan close <p>\` and create it again (a Plan cannot be changed after it is created)
7. When a human approves, record the approval with \`dg --json --actor agent plan approve <p>\` (\`approved_by\` is the acting actor). Do not approve without a human's approval. Do not start tickets in a Plan that is awaiting approval
8. Advance an approved Plan following \`dg guide work\`. If you lose track of the Plan's ID, list open Plans with \`dg --json --actor agent plan list --status open\`, or find it from the \`plan\` of \`ticket show\` (the open Plan the ticket is in)

## Creating a Plan

\`\`\`sh
dg --json --actor agent plan create t12 t13 t14 t15 \\
  --after t13=t12 --after t14=t12 --after t15=t13,t14 \\
  --scope t13="API for listing and detail. Authentication is not included" \\
  --approach t13="Add core command definitions, and HTTP only passes them through" \\
  --tier t12=light --tier t13=standard --tier t15=heavy
\`\`\`

- The order you list them is a guide to the execution order. \`--after t15=t13,t14\` means "advance t15 after t13 and t14". It is a wait within the Plan only (separate from the ticket's \`depends_on\`). Tickets that can run in parallel do not write after for each other
- Write \`--scope\` and \`--approach\` in one sentence per ticket. Read them back with \`details\` from \`plan show\` when starting the ticket
- \`--tier\` is the ticket's difficulty level, and decides which model implements it (\`models.*\` in \`config.yaml\`. It appears as \`model\` in \`dg next\`). light: only documents, settings, and wording. heavy: design decisions, data shapes, spanning several packages, or a bug whose cause is unknown. standard: everything else. When in doubt, choose the heavier one. If omitted, it is treated the same as standard
- Tickets that are done or abandoned, and tickets in another open Plan (those whose \`plan\` in \`ticket show\` is not empty), cannot be added (\`REJECTED\`). If one is in another Plan, ask a human whether that Plan may be closed
- If dependencies are missing, fix them with \`dg --json --actor agent ticket update <id> --depends-on t12,t13\`. \`--depends-on\` **replaces** the whole list, so specify every dependency you keep (\`--label\` works the same way). Cycles are refused
- If new work is found while planning, do not advance it on your own. Propose filing it by the steps of \`dg guide import\`

## About approval

By default, agents can also run \`plan approve\`. To limit it to humans, add \`plan.approve\` to \`actor.human_only\` in \`config.yaml\` (see \`dg guide overview\`). If it is refused (\`TRANSITION_REJECTED\`), ask a human to run \`dg plan approve <p>\`.

## Principles of progress

- Claim only the one ticket you can advance right now. Do not claim everything first (others, or other agents, could not start)
- Do not start a ticket whose dependency is neither done nor abandoned. If the Workflow writes \`requires: [deps_done]\` for todo → in_progress, claim is refused (\`TRANSITION_REJECTED\`). If it does not, wait until the ticket appears in \`--ready\`, or advance the dependency first
- A Plan's \`after\` is not checked at claim. Wait until \`items[].waiting\` in \`plan show\` (the waits on \`after\` and \`depends_on\` that are not finished) is empty

## Asking with choices

Tickets advance in this order: import (\`dg guide import\`) → assign (\`dg guide assign\`) → check (\`dg guide check\`) → plan (\`dg guide plan\`) → work (\`dg guide work\`) → audit (\`dg guide audit\`, when an Iteration or Epic ends) → release (\`dg guide release\`; if there are no release steps, clean up with \`dg guide done\`). Done (\`dg guide done\`) comes after every merge. To carry on from assign through release, use \`dg guide all\`. When asking a human for a decision (approval, correction, which one to choose), and when a step is finished and the next step must be decided, show the choices and ask.

- When the agent can offer choices, ask with that mechanism (in Claude Code, the AskUserQuestion tool). If it cannot, show the numbered choices in text
- Show the material for the decision (tables, Plans, lists of PRs) before the choices. Do not ask for a decision with only the choices' descriptions
- Offer 2 to 4 choices, put the recommended one first, and give its reason. An answer that cannot be a choice, such as the content of a correction, is taken as free input outside the choices
- Ask questions where several can be chosen (such as which lines to reflect) with multiple selection. Ask about deletion, abandoned, and close operations one at a time
- When deciding the next step, make the next step in the flow and "stop here" the choices (see the "Choosing the next step" section of each guide). When the next step is chosen, if a Skill is available (for example \`/dgwork p3\`), start it. If not, read \`dg guide <topic>\` and continue with that step. Do not do the next step's work inside the current step.

## Choosing the next step

After approving a Plan (including when an approved Plan was specified), ask with these choices. When you stop while the Plan is still awaiting approval, do not ask.

- **Advance the Plan** (recommended): advance it by the steps of \`dg guide work\`, specifying the Plan's ID (if a Skill is available, \`/dgwork p3\`). The one advancing starts from the Plan's record (\`plan show\`)
- **Stop here**: another session advances it by specifying the Plan's ID

When you only show the order, ask with "Make a plan" (the steps of \`dg guide plan\`; if a Skill is available, \`/dgplan next <N>\`), "Assign" (if there are unassigned tickets, the steps of \`dg guide assign\`; if a Skill is available, \`/dgassign\`), and "Stop here"."
`;

